THOUGHT: NullAway complains because getAdapterFromObject accepts a @Nullable T and immediately dereferences it via object.getClass(). I should confirm how this method is used to ensure object is never actually null, then either (a) enforce non-null at the call sites or (b) add a defensive null check here. Since evictToSize() uses Preconditions.checkNotNull(evicted) before calling getAdapterFromObject(evicted), object should be non-null in practice. The minimal, safe fix is to add a null check in getAdapterFromObject that throws a clear exception if object is null, which both satisfies NullAway and avoids changing other behavior. After editing, I need to run the Gradle compile task and save the output to verify that the total NullAway error count decreased by exactly one and that no new errors were introduced.

```bash
cd /home/vscode/nullness-benchmarks/glide && sed -i '188,192c\  @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
  private <T> ArrayAdapterInterface<T> getAdapterFromObject(@Nullable T object) {
    if (object == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException("Array object must not be null");
    }
    return (ArrayAdapterInterface<T>) getAdapterFromType(object.getClass());
  }' library/src/main/java/com/bumptech/glide/load/engine/bitmap_recycle/LruArrayPool.java && ./gradlew library:compileDebugJavaWithJavac -x test -q > new_build_output.log 2>&1 && grep -E 'NullAway:|error\(s\)' new_build_output.log | tail -n 20
```

